# Answer 4 · The Development and Stance of Marxist/Socialist Feminism (CSS 2021)
@py question 2021-4

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Two limbs: trace the development in stages, then set out the stance on gender and exploitation — and end with Pakistan.
@ask ① DISCUSS the DEVELOPMENT of Marxist/Socialist feminism
@ask ② its PHILOSOPHICAL STANCE on gender issues and EXPLOITATION
(1) Introduction: oppression rooted in capitalism and private property; Engels 1884; later joined by patriarchy
(2) Marxist groundwork: capitalism, class, exploitation, base and superstructure, reserve army
(3) Classical stage: Engels, Bebel, Zetkin and Kollontai — property, monogamy and women's paid work
(4) Domestic-labour debate: Benston, Dalla Costa and James, Federici — unpaid housework produces the worker
(5) Socialist turn: Mitchell's four structures; Hartmann's dual systems; Eisenstein and Young
(6) The stance: materialism; two exploitations; double burden; family serves capital; socialised care
(7) Exploitation explained: unpaid care, cheap paid labour, the reserve army, global factories
(8) Criticisms: sex-blind; reduces gender to class; violence underplayed; socialist states failed women
(9) Pakistan: unpaid farm work, home-based workers, Lady Health Workers, BISP
(10) Conclusion: rights on paper need economic independence and shared care
:::

## Decoding the question
The question gives its own definition — use it in your introduction. It has **two limbs**. **"Discuss the development"** asks for **stages over time**, with thinkers and dates. **"The philosophical stance … on gender issues and exploitation"** asks for the school's **core beliefs** — why women are oppressed and how they are exploited. The slash in "Marxist/Socialist" invites you to show how **socialist feminism grew out of Marxist feminism** and how they differ.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Marxist feminism** holds that women are exploited through **capitalism and private property**. **Socialist feminism** accepts this but adds a second system, **patriarchy**, which works together with capitalism.

#### ii) Origin
The school began with **Friedrich Engels**'s *The Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State* (==1884==) and grew through the **domestic-labour debate** of the ==1970s==.

#### iii) The argument
Its lasting contribution is to show that women's disadvantage is **material** — rooted in who does **paid and unpaid work** and who **owns property**. Legal equality means little without **economic independence**.

### 2. The Marxist groundwork

#### i) Five terms
**Capitalism**: a few own the means of production; most sell their labour. **Class**: owners (bourgeoisie) and workers (proletariat). **Exploitation**: workers produce more value than they are paid; the owner keeps the **profit**. **Base and superstructure**: the economy shapes the family, law and ideas. **Reserve army of labour**: workers hired in booms and dropped in slumps.

#### ii) Why these matter
Marxist feminists applied these terms to women — asking what **work** women do, who **profits** from it, and why it is **unpaid**.

### 3. Stage one — classical Marxist feminism (1879–1920s)

#### i) Engels
Engels argued that when men began to own **private property**, they wanted to pass it to **their own sons**. To be certain of paternity, they imposed **monogamy on women** and took control of the household — "**the world historical defeat of the female sex**". Women will be free when private property ends and they enter **paid production** as equals.

#### ii) Bebel, Zetkin and Kollontai
**August Bebel** (*Woman and Socialism*, ==1879==) linked women's and workers' struggles. **Clara Zetkin** proposed **International Women's Day** at Copenhagen in ==1910==. **Alexandra Kollontai** pushed for **communal kitchens and childcare** in the Soviet Union after ==1917==.

### 4. Stage two — the domestic-labour debate (1969–1975)

#### i) Housework as production
**Margaret Benston** (==1969==) argued that housework is real **production** — meals, clean clothes, cared-for children — but, being unpaid, is not counted as work.

#### ii) Producing the worker
**Mariarosa Dalla Costa and Selma James** (==1972==) argued that housework produces the most important commodity of all: **the worker** himself, fed and rested each morning. This launched the **Wages for Housework** campaign (==1972==). **Silvia Federici** (==1975==) wrote: "They say it is love. We say it is **unwaged work**."

#### iii) Social reproduction
This work of keeping people alive and able to work is called **social reproduction**. Capitalism depends on it but does not pay for it.

::: fig cycle | How women's unpaid work keeps capitalism running.
@centre Capitalist profit
Housework: a fed, rested worker each morning
Childcare: the next generation of workers
Care of sick and old: at no cost to the state
Male "breadwinner" wage: women depend on it
Cheap female labour: hired when needed, dropped when not
:::

### 5. Stage three — the socialist turn (1966–1990)

#### i) Mitchell
**Juliet Mitchell** ("Women: The Longest Revolution", ==1966==; *Woman's Estate*, ==1971==) said women's position rests on **four structures**: production, reproduction, sexuality and the socialisation of children. Changing the economy alone will not free women.

#### ii) Hartmann
**Heidi Hartmann** ("The Unhappy Marriage of Marxism and Feminism", ==1979==) called Marxism "**sex-blind**": it explains why workers are exploited, but not why **men** dominate **women** in every class. Her **dual-systems theory** treats capitalism and patriarchy as **two linked systems**. Her example: male trade unions won a **family wage** for men, pushing women back into the home.

#### iii) Eisenstein, Young, Mies and Walby
**Zillah Eisenstein** (==1979==) coined "**capitalist patriarchy**". **Iris Marion Young** (==1980==) argued for one **unified system**. **Maria Mies** (==1986==) described the "**housewifisation**" of women's work worldwide. **Sylvia Walby** (==1990==) identified **six structures** of patriarchy.

### 6. The philosophical stance

#### i) Materialism
Women's position is explained by **material conditions** — work, property, money — not by nature.

#### ii) The family serves capital
The family produces and maintains workers **cheaply** and passes on **property**.

#### iii) The double burden
Women who take paid work still do the housework — the "**second shift**" (**Arlie Hochschild**, ==1989==).

#### iv) Two exploitations, one system
Women are exploited as **unpaid workers** at home and as **low-paid workers** outside it. The two are linked: because housework is assumed to be women's duty, employers treat women's wages as "extra" and pay them less.

#### v) The remedy
Liberation needs **economic independence** and **socialised care**: public childcare, paid leave, equal pay. Marxists add the end of capitalism; socialists add the end of male privilege at home and work.

### 7. Exploitation explained

::: table | The four forms of exploitation in Marxist/socialist feminism.
@widths 3.6,10.4
Form | Meaning and example
Unpaid domestic labour | Cooking, cleaning and care that keep workers alive, done free
Cheap paid labour | "Women's jobs" — stitching, cleaning, nursing — paid less
Reserve army | Women hired in booms or wars and pushed out in slumps
Global assembly line | Young women in export factories of the global South, paid little
:::

### 8. Criticisms

#### i) Sex-blind
Marxism cannot explain **male power within the working class** (Hartmann).

#### ii) Too economic
It underplays **violence, sexuality and culture** — the concerns of radical feminism.

#### iii) The socialist record
Women in the **Soviet Union** entered paid work in large numbers but still carried a **double burden** at home.

#### iv) Wages for housework
Critics feared that paying for housework would **fix women in the home** rather than share the work with men.

#### v) The reply
Socialist feminists accept much of this criticism — which is precisely why they added **patriarchy** to capitalism. Today's **care-economy** debates, and **SDG target 5.4** on recognising unpaid care, show the school's continuing influence.

### 9. Pakistan

#### i) Unpaid and cheap work
Female labour-force participation is only ==24.4%== (LFS 2024–25). Most employed women work in **agriculture**, much as **unpaid family helpers**. Millions of **home-based workers** earn piece rates; **Sindh** passed the first **Home-Based Workers Act** in ==2018==.

#### ii) Organised struggle
The **Lady Health Workers** (programme from ==1994==) fought for regular jobs, winning a Supreme Court order in ==2012==. Rural women of the **Sindhiani Tehreek** (from ==1982==) and the **Okara** tenants' movement (from ==2000==) joined land and class struggles.

#### iii) Policy
The **Benazir Income Support Programme** (==2008==) pays cash to **women** — a small recognition of their role in family survival.

### 10. Conclusion
Marxist/socialist feminism developed from **Engels's** theory of property, through the **domestic-labour debate**, to the **dual-systems** theory of Hartmann. Its stance is that women are exploited **twice** — as unpaid carers and as cheap workers — by **capitalism and patriarchy together**. Its critics are right that economics is not everything. But for Pakistan, where most women's work is **unpaid or underpaid**, its lesson is essential: rights on paper need **economic independence and shared care** to become rights in life.

## Why this answer scores
- The question's **own definition** opens the answer.
- **Development** is shown in **three dated stages**, and **stance** under its own heading — both limbs answered.
- A **cycle figure** and a **table** make the argument of exploitation visible.
- **Criticisms** are fair, and the **Pakistan** section uses current data and real movements.
